WEBVTT

00:00.636 --> 00:02.517
- [Instructor] We're told
that a certain mapping

00:02.517 --> 00:06.766
in the xy-plane has the
following two properties.

00:06.766 --> 00:09.552
Each point on the line y is equal to

00:09.552 --> 00:12.552
three x minus two maps to itself.

00:13.569 --> 00:15.938
Any point P not on the line

00:15.938 --> 00:17.771
maps to a new point P'

00:18.771 --> 00:22.509
in such a way that the
perpendicular bisector of

00:22.509 --> 00:25.504
the segment PP' is the line

00:25.504 --> 00:29.220
y is equal to three x minus two.

00:29.220 --> 00:31.031
Which of the following statements is true?

00:31.031 --> 00:33.283
So is this describing a
reflection, a rotation,

00:33.283 --> 00:35.025
or a translation?

00:35.025 --> 00:37.114
So pause this video and see
if you can work through it

00:37.114 --> 00:38.114
on your own.

00:39.181 --> 00:42.246
All right so let me just
try to visualize this.

00:42.246 --> 00:45.149
So, and I'll just do a very quick,

00:45.149 --> 00:47.447
so if that's my y-axis,

00:47.447 --> 00:50.907
and that this right
over here is my x-axis.

00:50.907 --> 00:54.088
Three x minus two might
look something like this.

00:54.088 --> 00:55.969
The line three x minus two would look

00:55.969 --> 00:57.339
something like that.

00:57.339 --> 00:58.686
And so what we're saying is,

00:58.686 --> 00:59.986
or what they're telling us,

00:59.986 --> 01:01.728
is any point on this
after the transformation

01:01.728 --> 01:03.539
maps to itself.

01:03.539 --> 01:06.047
Now that by itself is a pretty good clue

01:06.047 --> 01:08.090
that we're likely dealing
with a reflection.

01:08.090 --> 01:09.297
Because remember with a reflection

01:09.297 --> 01:11.434
you reflect over a line,

01:11.434 --> 01:13.245
but if a point sits on the line,

01:13.245 --> 01:16.310
well it's just gonna
continue to sit on the line.

01:16.310 --> 01:18.167
But let's just make sure
that the second point

01:18.167 --> 01:20.722
is consistent with it being a reflection.

01:20.722 --> 01:23.508
So any point P not on the line,

01:23.508 --> 01:26.712
so let's see, point P, right over here,

01:26.712 --> 01:29.081
it maps to a new point P' in such a way

01:29.081 --> 01:32.308
that the perpendicular bisector of PP'

01:32.308 --> 01:35.489
is the line y equals three x minus two.

01:35.489 --> 01:37.115
So I need to connect,

01:37.115 --> 01:38.032
so the line

01:39.530 --> 01:40.900
three x minus two,

01:40.900 --> 01:42.525
y is equal to three x minus two,

01:42.525 --> 01:44.383
would be the perpendicular bisector

01:44.383 --> 01:46.890
of the segment between P and what?

01:46.890 --> 01:50.002
Well let's see I'd have to
draw a perpendicular line.

01:50.002 --> 01:52.928
It would have to have the
same length on both sides

01:52.928 --> 01:55.598
of the line y equals three x minus two.

01:55.598 --> 01:58.524
So P' would have to be right over there.

01:58.524 --> 02:02.378
So once again this is consistent
with being a reflection.

02:02.378 --> 02:06.976
P' is equidistant on the
other side of the line as P.

02:06.976 --> 02:09.414
So I definitely feel good that this

02:09.414 --> 02:12.943
is going to be a
reflection right over here.

02:12.943 --> 02:15.358
Let's do another example.

02:15.358 --> 02:17.518
So here we are told,

02:17.518 --> 02:19.259
and I'll switch my colors up,

02:19.259 --> 02:20.931
a certain mapping in the
plane has the following

02:20.931 --> 02:21.929
two properties.

02:21.929 --> 02:24.275
Point O maps to itself.

02:24.275 --> 02:27.131
Every point V on a circle C

02:27.131 --> 02:28.298
centered at O,

02:29.545 --> 02:32.796
all right, maps to a new point W

02:32.796 --> 02:33.713
on circle C

02:35.792 --> 02:39.716
so that the counterclockwise
angle from segment OV

02:39.716 --> 02:42.433
to OW measures 137 degrees.

02:42.433 --> 02:44.778
So is this a reflection,
rotation, or translation?

02:44.778 --> 02:48.330
Pause this video and try to
figure it out on your own.

02:48.330 --> 02:50.560
All right, so let's see.

02:50.560 --> 02:53.160
We're talking about circle

02:53.160 --> 02:54.577
centered at O.

02:54.577 --> 02:55.807
So let's see, let me just say,

02:55.807 --> 02:57.595
so I have this point O.

02:57.595 --> 03:00.103
It maps to itself on its transformation.

03:00.103 --> 03:03.586
Now every point V on
circle C centered at O.

03:03.586 --> 03:05.420
So let's see, let's say this is circle C

03:05.420 --> 03:07.789
centered at point O,

03:07.789 --> 03:10.807
so I'm gonna try to draw a
decent looking circle here.

03:10.807 --> 03:12.665
You get the idea.

03:12.665 --> 03:16.125
This is not the best hand-drawn
circle ever, all right.

03:16.125 --> 03:18.981
So every point, let's
just pick a point V here.

03:18.981 --> 03:21.651
So let's say that that is the point V,

03:21.651 --> 03:24.786
on a circle centered at
O maps to a new point W

03:24.786 --> 03:26.434
on the circle C.

03:26.434 --> 03:28.803
So maybe it maps to a new point W on,

03:28.803 --> 03:30.242
actually let me keep reading,

03:30.242 --> 03:33.958
W on circle C so that the
counterclockwise angle

03:33.958 --> 03:35.041
from OV to OW

03:36.442 --> 03:38.671
measures 137 degrees.

03:38.671 --> 03:41.921
Okay so we wanna know the angle from OV

03:42.781 --> 03:45.114
to OW going counterclockwise

03:45.962 --> 03:47.212
is 137 degrees.

03:49.260 --> 03:52.510
So this right over here is 137 degrees.

03:53.370 --> 03:57.201
And so this would be the segment OW.

03:57.201 --> 03:59.871
W would go right over there.

03:59.871 --> 04:02.541
And so what this looks like is well

04:02.541 --> 04:05.003
if we're talking about angles

04:05.003 --> 04:07.580
and we are rotating something,

04:07.580 --> 04:09.531
this point corresponds to this point,

04:09.531 --> 04:12.062
it's essentially the
point has been rotated

04:12.062 --> 04:13.229
by 137 degrees

04:14.337 --> 04:15.587
around point O.

04:16.729 --> 04:20.562
So this right over here
is clearly a rotation.

04:21.489 --> 04:22.696
This is a rotation.

04:22.696 --> 04:24.391
Sometimes reading this language at first

04:24.391 --> 04:25.854
is a little bit daunting.

04:25.854 --> 04:27.317
It was a little bit
daunting to me when I first

04:27.317 --> 04:28.734
(laughing) read it.

04:28.734 --> 04:30.730
But when you actually just break it down

04:30.730 --> 04:33.795
